Two sources that share one hashed token fail the build. The message names
both sources and both files. This holds for ctx, value, name and id.

  $ cat > ctx_a.ml <<EOT
  > [@@@css.atoms [("_c0aXXXXXXYYYYYY", ["a{}"], 2, 1, 1, [("ctx", "XXXXXX", "&:hover")])]]
  > EOT

  $ cat > ctx_b.ml <<EOT
  > [@@@css.atoms [("_c0bXXXXXXZZZZZZ", ["b{}"], 2, 1, 1, [("ctx", "XXXXXX", "&:focus")])]]
  > EOT

  $ styled-ppx.generate ctx_a.ml ctx_b.ml
  styled-ppx: ctx hash collision on `XXXXXX`: "&:hover" in ctx_a.ml and "&:focus" in ctx_b.ml
  [1]

  $ cat > value_a.ml <<EOT
  > [@@@css.atoms [("_a0aVVVVVV", ["a{}"], 1, 0, 1, [("value", "VVVVVV", "color:red")])]]
  > EOT

  $ cat > value_b.ml <<EOT
  > [@@@css.atoms [("_a0bVVVVVV", ["b{}"], 1, 0, 1, [("value", "VVVVVV", "color:blue")])]]
  > EOT

  $ styled-ppx.generate value_a.ml value_b.ml
  styled-ppx: value hash collision on `VVVVVV`: "color:red" in value_a.ml and "color:blue" in value_b.ml
  [1]

  $ cat > name_a.ml <<EOT
  > [@@@css.atoms [("_xNNNNNNaaaaaaVVVVVV", ["a{}"], 1, 0, 1, [("name", "NNNNNN", "--one")])]]
  > EOT

  $ cat > name_b.ml <<EOT
  > [@@@css.atoms [("_xNNNNNNbbbbbbWWWWWW", ["b{}"], 1, 0, 1, [("name", "NNNNNN", "--two")])]]
  > EOT

  $ styled-ppx.generate name_a.ml name_b.ml
  styled-ppx: name hash collision on `NNNNNN`: "--one" in name_a.ml and "--two" in name_b.ml
  [1]

  $ cat > id_a.ml <<EOT
  > [@@@css.atoms [("_iIIIIIIII", ["a{}"], 1, 0, 1, [("id", "IIIIIIII", "bind Lib A top x 0")])]]
  > EOT

  $ cat > id_b.ml <<EOT
  > [@@@css.atoms [("_iIIIIIIII", ["a{}"], 1, 0, 1, [("id", "IIIIIIII", "bind Lib B top x 0")])]]
  > EOT

  $ styled-ppx.generate id_a.ml id_b.ml
  styled-ppx: id hash collision on `IIIIIIII`: "bind Lib A top x 0" in id_a.ml and "bind Lib B top x 0" in id_b.ml
  [1]

The same source behind one token, in two files, is not a collision.

  $ styled-ppx.generate ctx_a.ml ctx_a.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  a{}

The ppx writes a minted binding id as an atom with no rules, so a binding that
holds no atom is checked too. Two bindings with one id token fail the build.

  $ cat > bind_a.ml <<EOT
  > [@@@css.atoms [("_iQQQQQQQQ", [], 1, 0, 0, [("id", "_iQQQQQQQQ", "bind  A  m 0")])]]
  > EOT

  $ cat > bind_b.ml <<EOT
  > [@@@css.atoms [("_iQQQQQQQQ", [], 1, 0, 0, [("id", "_iQQQQQQQQ", "bind  B  m 0")])]]
  > EOT

  $ styled-ppx.generate bind_a.ml bind_b.ml
  styled-ppx: id hash collision on `_iQQQQQQQQ`: "bind  A  m 0" in bind_a.ml and "bind  B  m 0" in bind_b.ml
  [1]
